            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, thank you for referring this 90-year-old male patient who was admitted on 27/11/2006 with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray confirmed hyperinflation without consolidation. Arterial blood gases revealed hypoxaemia with mild hypercapnia. Blood tests showed elevated inflammatory markers. He was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and intravenous fluids. Clinical response was satisfactory with improvement in oxygen saturation and symptom relief. He was discharged on 28/11/2006 in a stable condition with oral prednisolone taper and inhaler optimisation. Please arrange early follow-up and consider pulmonary rehabilitation referral. Do not hesitate to contact me with any queries.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
